{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What is a variable's scope?",
      "options": [
        "The part of the code where the variable exists and can be used",
        "The kind of value it holds",
        "How many times its value can change",
        "The line where it was first written"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "def greet():\n    message = \"Hello from Vice City\"\n    print(message)\n\ngreet()\nprint(message)",
      "options": [
        "It prints Hello from Vice City twice: message stays after the call",
        "It prints Hello from Vice City, then stops with a NameError: message exists only inside greet",
        "It prints nothing: greet() only stores the message",
        "It stops before printing anything: print() can't be used inside a function"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "Where can a variable created inside a function be used?",
      "options": [
        "Anywhere in the program, once the function has run",
        "Only on the line right after the call",
        "Inside that function and in the functions written after it",
        "Only inside that function"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "Which variables can a function read without getting them as parameters?",
      "options": [
        "None: a function sees only its parameters",
        "The local variables of the other functions",
        "Global ones, created outside any function",
        "Only the ones created after the function was called"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "name = \"Tommy Vercetti\"\n\ndef greet():\n    print(f\"Hello, {name}\")\n\ngreet()\nprint(name)",
      "options": [
        "Hello, Tommy Vercetti\nTommy Vercetti",
        "Hello, {name}\nTommy Vercetti",
        "It stops with a NameError",
        "Hello, Tommy Vercetti"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "health = 100\n\ndef take_damage():\n    health = health - 10\n    print(health)\n\ntake_damage()",
      "options": [
        "It prints 90: the function takes the global health and subtracts 10",
        "It prints 100: the change is lost when the function ends",
        "It prints 90 and the global health becomes 90 too",
        "It stops with an error: health = ... makes health local, and the local one has no value yet"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "When does Python treat a variable inside a function as local?",
      "options": [
        "When the function gives it a value with =, even if a global has the same name",
        "Only when no global has its name",
        "Only when it is a parameter",
        "Never: inside a function Python always uses the global"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "health = 100\n\ndef take_damage(hp):\n    hp = hp - 10\n    return hp\n\nhealth = take_damage(health)\nprint(health)",
      "options": ["100", "It stops with an error", "80", "90"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "What does the lesson call the best way for a function to work with data?",
      "options": [
        "Read and change the global variables directly",
        "Print its results instead of returning them",
        "Take data through parameters and send results back with return",
        "Give every variable in the program a different name"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def f():\n    x = 5\n    print(x)\n\ndef g():\n    x = 99\n    print(x)\n\nf()\ng()",
      "options": ["99\n99", "5\n5", "5\n99", "It stops with an error: x is made twice"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "`f` sets `x = 5` and `g` sets `x = 99`. Why don't the two x get in each other's way?",
      "options": [
        "Each one is local to its own function, so they are two different variables",
        "Python keeps only the last x it saw",
        "g runs after f, so it erases f's x first",
        "They do: the second x replaces the first one for good"
      ],
      "answer": 0
